{"id":"7cd0aaaa731498372e1001e00e5fecb7be03096e19e5a1658a049c43e1f94142","lamport":1,"author":"scual.project@gmail.com","created_at":"2026-11-08T20:21:42.759647658Z","format_version":1,"kind":"set-field","payload":{"fields":{"description":"## Parent\n\nSub-issue of 215ee3.\n\n## What to build\n\nA maintainer can repair a sub-issue or dependency link from the CLI whenever the two sides disagree. `ti subissue <parent> -t <child>` and `ti subissue --clear -t <child>` succeed when the child's parent field is set but the parent's children set lacks the child (today both fail with \"value not found\"). `ti depends --remove` succeeds when the reverse set already lacks the entry. Removing an absent set member is a success in the store, never an error. `ti verify` reports every parent/children and depends-on/blocks asymmetry as an issue.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] CLI test: link whose parent's children set lacks the child; `ti subissue --clear` exits 0 and clears; relinking exits 0 and both sides agree\n- [ ] CLI test: `ti depends --remove` on a half-present dependency exits 0 and both sides agree\n- [ ] CLI test: `ti verify` on a repo with a link asymmetry prints one issue per asymmetry naming both tickets, non-zero exit; clean repo unchanged\n- [ ] Existing test suites green"}}}